<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wabash River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wabash River flows about 810 kilometres from western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> across Indiana to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a>, forming part of the border between Indiana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>. Its valley was home to the Miami and other Native peoples and was settled by the French at Vincennes. In 1791 the Western Confederacy routed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-st-clair">Arthur St. Clair</a>'s army near its headwaters, and in 1808 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenskwatawa">Tenskwatawa</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophetstown">Prophetstown</a> near its junction with the Tippecanoe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wabash-river.jpg" alt="Wabash River" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kmusser · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wabashrivermap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Wabash drains most of Indiana and a significant part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>. It rises about four miles south of Fort Recovery, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, from farmland drainage, flows north and then sharply west into Indiana, crosses northern Indiana to the south-west and turns south near the Illinois border, where its southern part forms the boundary between the two states. It is the largest northern tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a> and the third largest overall, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>. Its major tributaries are the Tippecanoe, White, Embarras and Little Wabash rivers. The name is the English form of the French Ouabache, which adopted the Miami-Illinois word waapaahšiiki, meaning water over white stones, since the river bottom in Huntington County is white limestone.</p><p>As the Laurentide Ice Sheet retreated 14,000 to 15,000 years ago, meltwater formed proglacial Lake Maumee. Around 11,000 years ago it broke through the Fort Wayne Moraine and drained catastrophically, scouring a valley one to two miles wide, the Wabash-Erie Channel. For part of its course the Wabash follows the path of the pre-glacial Teays River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>French explorers first mapped the river to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> in the latter half of the 17th century. Until the mid-18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> was considered a tributary of the Wabash, and French traders used it as a vital route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. In the 18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miami-people">Miami people</a> controlled at Kekionga the profitable 8-mile portage between the Maumee and the Wabash.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> fought five colonial and frontier-era battles on or near the river: Vincennes in 1779, St. Clair's Defeat in 1791, the Attack on Fort Recovery in 1794, Tippecanoe in 1811 and the Siege of Fort Harrison in 1812. In the mid-19th century the Wabash and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal">Erie Canal</a>, one of the longest in the world, was built along much of the river, but railroads surpassed it and most of the canal no longer exists. The river below Terre Haute was navigable by large ships during much of the 19th century, with steamships calling regularly. By the late 19th century erosion from farming and runoff had made it impassable to them, and dredging was not undertaken because railroads were preferred.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river is about 503 miles long, or 810 kilometres, and flows freely for 411 miles from the dam near Huntington, Indiana, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>. It is the state river of Indiana and the subject of the state song, On the Banks of the Wabash, Far Away, by Paul Dresser. Counties, townships, colleges and a canal bear its name, as did four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">US Navy</a> warships. A 329-acre remnant of the old-growth forests that bordered it survives at Beall Woods State Park, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-carmel">Mount Carmel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wabash River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1808 he and his brother Tecumseh founded Prophetstown near the junction of the Tippecanoe and <b>Wabash</b> rivers, drawing followers from many nations.</blockquote><a href="/state/tecumsehs-confederacy">Tecumseh's Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It defeated Josiah Harmar in 1790 and destroyed Arthur St. Clair's army on the <b>Wabash</b> in 1791.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-confederacy">Western Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
